ACC-002 — Canonical Definitions

Document ID: ACC-002
Title: Definitions
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification establishes the rules governing the definition and use of concepts within the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

Its purpose is to ensure that every concept has a single, canonical meaning that remains consistent across all Atlas specifications, implementations, and future revisions.

2. Scope

This specification applies to all concepts defined within the Atlas Core Concepts (ACC) and to every specification that references them, including but not limited to:

Atlas Hivemind Specification (AHS)
Engineering Understanding Specification (EUS)
Engineering Memory Specification (EMS)
Engineering Knowledge Specification (EKS)
Engineering Evidence Specification (EES)
Engineering Reasoning Specification (ERS)
Engineering Capability Specification (ECS)
Decision Support Specification (DSS)
3. Canonical Definition

A canonical definition is the single authoritative description of a concept within the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework.

Each concept SHALL have exactly one canonical definition.

No specification SHALL redefine a concept in a way that changes its canonical meaning.

4. Definition Principles

All definitions SHALL conform to the following principles.

4.1 Uniqueness

Each concept SHALL have one and only one canonical definition.

4.2 Clarity

Definitions SHALL be precise, unambiguous, and understandable without relying on implementation-specific terminology.

4.3 Consistency

A concept SHALL retain the same meaning throughout every Atlas specification.

4.4 Technology Independence

Definitions SHALL not depend upon any specific software, programming language, database, artificial intelligence model, hardware platform, or implementation technology.

4.5 Domain Independence

Definitions SHALL remain applicable across engineering disciplines unless explicitly stated otherwise.

4.6 Extensibility

New concepts MAY be introduced without modifying the canonical meaning of previously defined concepts.

4.7 Traceability

Every defined concept SHALL explicitly identify its relationships to other Atlas concepts where applicable.

5. Definition Structure

Every core concept defined by the Atlas Core Concepts SHALL follow the same structure.

Each concept specification SHALL contain the following sections:

Definition
Purpose
Characteristics
Relationships
Responsibilities
Normative Statements
Non-Goals

This standardized structure ensures consistency and simplifies cross-referencing throughout the Atlas specification suite.

6. Normative Requirements

The following requirements apply to all Atlas specifications.

Every core concept SHALL be defined only within the Atlas Core Concepts (ACC).
Other specifications SHALL reference canonical definitions instead of redefining them.
Changes to canonical definitions SHALL be managed through formal version control and specification revision.
Definitions SHALL remain implementation-independent.
Definitions SHALL remain internally consistent with all related concepts.
7. Conformance

A specification conforms to ACC-002 if it:

References canonical Atlas definitions.
Does not redefine established concepts.
Uses terminology consistently.
Preserves the intended meaning of referenced concepts.
8. Non-Goals

This specification does not:

Define engineering processes.
Define software architecture.
Define implementation details.
Define algorithms or data structures.
Replace domain-specific engineering standards.
9. Future Evolution

The Atlas Core Concepts are expected to evolve as the framework matures.

Future revisions MAY introduce new concepts or refine existing definitions, provided that:

Backward compatibility is preserved where practical.
Canonical meanings remain stable.
Changes are documented through formal specification versioning.
Dependent specifications remain traceable to the revised definitions.